@c page
@node overview
@chapter Overview of the package


This document describes version @value{VERSION} of @value{PACKAGE}, a
modern embeddable key--value database designed for a high load
environment.

It has a unique architecture that was born as a result of research and
rethinking primary alghorithmical constraints associated with a getting
popular Log--file based data structures, such as LSM--tree, its
variations based on Fractional Cascading ideas and a B-Tree.

It is written entirely in C and can be used in C/C++ applications or by
any interpreted language if the appropriate library bindings are
available.

Sophia supports the traditional set/get/delete operations semantic plus
range queries by using cursors.  Database @api{} has been designed to be
as clean as possible and easy to use.

All sophia @api{} declarations are stored in a separate include file:
@file{sophia.h}.

And the library to link with is @code{-lsophia} accordingly.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Conventions

All functions return either @code{0} on success, or @code{-1} on error.
The only exception is functions that return a pointer; in that case
@cnull{} indicates an error.

Any error description can be accessed through @cfunc{sp_error} function.

All created objects must be freed by @cfunc{sp_destroy} function.  The
only exception is the @cfunc{sp_get} function, which allocates the
returned value via @cfunc{malloc} (and should be freed using
@cfunc{free} accordingly) or using the allocator--free function
specified in @cfunc{sp_ctl}.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Database

To open or create a database the @cfunc{sp_open} function must be used
with a configuration object (environment) previously created using
@cfunc{sp_env}.  The @cfunc{sp_env} function must be intialized using
@cfunc{sp_ctl}.  Before opening, a database directory must be specified
by @cfunc{sp_ctl} with flag @code{SPDIR}.

@example
#include <sophia.h>
void *env = sp_env();
if (env == NULL) @{
        /* memory allocation error */
@}
int rc = sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
if (rc == -1) @{
        /* memory allocation error */
        printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
        sp_destroy(env);
@}
@end example

To create a database directory the @code{SPO_CREAT} flag must be passed
to the @cfunc{sp_ctl} function.

The @cfunc{sp_open} function allocates a database handle and does the
main job of creating a new or recovering an existing database.

@example
void *db = sp_open(env);
if (db == NULL) @{
        printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
        sp_destroy(env);
@}
@end example

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Operations

There are a number of operations available to write or read data.  All
functions accept first arguments as key and key size.

To insert a new key--value pair use @cfunc{sp_set}.

@example
char value[] = "hello world";
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        rc = sp_set(db, &key, sizeof(key), value, sizeof(value));
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_set: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        key++;
@}
@end example

To get a key use @cfunc{sp_get}.

@cfunc{sp_get} allocates a value, which should be freed afterwards using
the @cfunc{free} function (or by allocator specific function passed to
@cfunc{sp_ctl} with flag @code{SPALLOC}.

@example
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        void *value;
        size_t valuesize;
        int rc = sp_get(s, &key, sizeof(key), &value, &valuesize);
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_get: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", key, value);
        free(value);
        key++;
@}
@end example

Please note that it is much faster to use range queries for sequential
key reads.

To delete a key use @cfunc{sp_delete}.

@example
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        rc = sp_delete(s, &key, sizeof(key));
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_delete: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        key++;
@}
@end example

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Range queries

It is possible to do range queries using cursors.

To create a cursor the @cfunc{sp_cursor} function should be used.
Cursor creation function allows to specify a query order and a start
key.

Possible orders are:

@table @code
@item SPGTE
Increasing order, including supplied key.

@item SPGT
Increasing order, excluding supplied key.

@item SPLTE
Decreasing order, including supplied key.

@item SPLT
Decreasing order, excluding supplied key.
@end table

If no key is specified whole key range will be traversed.

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGTE, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
        printf("cursor failed: %s\n", sp_error(db));
        /* error */
@}
@end example

To do a single step of iteration @cfunc{sp_fetch} function should be
used.  It returns @code{1} if there is more data available and @code{0}
otherwise:

@example
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
        char *k = sp_key(c);
        char *v = sp_value(c);
        printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", *(int*)k, v);
@}
@end example

To get current key or value data, @cfunc{sp_key}, @cfunc{sp_keysize} and
@cfunc{sp_value}, @cfunc{sp_valuesize} functions should be used
accordingly.

Cursor objects should be freed using the @cfunc{sp_destroy} function
after a usage.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Example

@example
#include <sophia.h>
int main(void)
@{
        /* create environment handle */
        void *env = sp_env();
        if (env == NULL)
                return 0;
        /* set database directory with it's open flags */
        int rc = sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
        if (rc == -1) @{
                sp_destroy(env);
                return 0;
        @}
        /* open a existing or create the new database */
        void *db = sp_open(env);
        if (db == NULL) @{
                printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
                sp_destroy(env);
                return 0;
        @}
        /* sequentially insert key-value pairs */
        char value[] = "hello world";
        int key = 0;
        while (key < 10) @{
                rc = sp_set(s, &key, sizeof(key), value, sizeof(value));
                if (rc == -1) @{
                        printf("sp_set: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                        goto err;
                @}
                key++;
        @}
        /* sequentially get key values.
         *
         * By default, order is defined by memcmp(3) function in
         * standart comparator. It is possible to specify custom comparator
         * in sp_ctl(SPCMP).
         */
        key = 0;
        while (key < 10) @{
                void *v;
                size_t vsize;
                rc = sp_get(s, &key, sizeof(key), &v, &vsize);
                if (rc == -1) @{
                        printf("sp_get: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                        goto err;
                @}
                printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", i, v);
                free(v);
                key++;
        @}
err:
        sp_destroy(db);
        sp_destroy(env);
        return 0;
@}
@end example

@c page
@node api
@chapter Programming interface


To use the library we have to include the single header file
@file{sophia.h}.


@deftypefun {void *} sp_env (void)
Create a new environment handle.  The @cfunc{sp_env} function allocates
new sophia environment object.  The object is intented for use by
@cfunc{sp_open} and must be initialized with @cfunc{sp_ctl} function
first.  After a use, the object should be free'd by @cfunc{sp_destroy}.

On success, @cfunc{sp_env} allocates new environment object; on error,
it returns @cnull{}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_ctl (void * @var{env}, spopt @var{opt}, @dots{})
Configure a database.  The @cfunc{sp_ctl} function sets database
environment parameter opt with some specific value:

Possible options are:

@table @code
@item SPDIR
Tail arguments: @code{uint32_t flags, char *path}.

Sets database directory path and it's open flags to use by
@cfunc{sp_open}.  Possible flags are:

@table @code
@item SPO_RDWR
Open repository in read--write mode (default).

@item SPO_RDONLY
Open repository in read--only mode.

@item SPO_CREAT
Create repository if it is not exists.
@end table

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
@end example

@item SPCMP
Tail arguments: @code{spcmpf f, void *arg}.

@example
typedef int (*spcmpf) (char *a, size_t asz,
                       char *b, size_t bsz,
                       void *arg);
@end example

Sets a database comparator function to use by database for a key order
determination.

The function must return: @code{0} if the keys are equal, @code{-1} if
key @var{a} is lower, @code{1} if key @var{b} is greater.

Example:

@example
int cmp(char *a, size_t asz, char *b, size_t bsz, void *arg) @{
    uint32_t av = *(uint32_t*)a;
    uint32_t bv = *(uint32_t*)b;
    if (av == bv)
       return 0;
    return (av > bv) ? 1 : -1;
@}
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPCMP, cmp, NULL);
@end example

@item SPALLOC
Tail arguments: @code{spallocf f, void *arg}.

@example
typedef void *(*spallocf)(void *ptr, size_t size, void *arg);
@end example

Sets a database allocator function to use instead of standard
@cfunc{malloc} allocator.  The allocator function will be used in all
internal allocations, except @cfunc{sp_env} call.

Example:

@example
void *alloc_func(void *ptr, size_t size, void *arg) @{
     /* allocate memory here */
@}
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPALLOC, alloc_func, NULL);
@end example

@item SPPAGE
Tail arguments: @code{uint32_t count}.

Sets database max key count in a single page.  This option can be
tweaked for performance.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPPAGE, 1024);
@end example

@item SPGC
Tail arguments: @code{int enabled}.

Sets flag that garbage collector should be turn on.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPGC, 1);
@end example

@item SPGCF
Tail arguments: @code{double factor}

Sets database garbage collector factor value, which is used to determine
whether it is time to start GC.

For example: a factor of @code{0.5} means that all ``alive'' pages from
any DB file will be copied to new DB, when there are half or less of
them is left.

This option can be tweaked for performance.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPGCF, 0.5);
@end example

@item SPGROW
Tail arguments: @code{uint32_t new_size, double resize}.

Sets the new database file's initial new size and resize factor.  These
values are used while database extend during merge.

This option can be tweaked for performance.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPGROW, 16 * 1024 * 1024, 2.0);
@end example

@item SPMERGE
Tail arguments: @code{int enabled}

Sets flag that merger thread must be created during @cfunc{sp_open}.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPMERGE, 1);
@end example

@item SPMERGEWM
Tail arguments: @code{uint32_t wm}

Sets database merge watermark value.

When database update count reaches this value, it notifies merger thread
to create a new epoch and start merge process of in--memory keys.

This option can be tweaked for performance.

Example:

@example
void *env = sp_env();
sp_ctl(env, SPMERGEWM, 200000);
@end example
@end table

On success, @cfunc{sp_ctl} returns @code{0}; on error, it returns
@code{-1}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun {void *} sp_open (void * @var{env})
Open or create a database.  The @cfunc{sp_open} function is used to open
or create a sophia database depending on supplied environment
configuration.

Example:

@example
void *env;
void *db;
int rc;
env = sp_env();
if (! env) @{
   /* memory allocation failed */
@}
rc = sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
if (rc == -1) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
@}
void *db = sp_open(env);
if (! db) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
@}
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_open} returns a database handle; on error, it
returns @cnull{}.  See @cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun {char *} sp_error (void * @var{ptr})
Get a string error description.  The @cfunc{sp_error} function is used
to get error description in a string format after any error occur.

Environment or database handle must be used.

Example:

@example
void *db = sp_open(env);
if (! db) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
@}
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_error} returns a string; if there is no error,
@cfunc{sp_error} returns @cnull{}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_destroy (void * @var{ptr})
Free any handle.  The @cfunc{sp_destroy} function is used to free memory
used by any sophia handle, such as: environment, database or cursor.

Example:

@example
void *db = sp_open(env);
/* ... */
int rc = sp_destroy(db);
if (rc == -1) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
@}
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_destroy} returns @code{0}; on error, it returns
@code{-1}.  See @cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_set (void * @var{db}, const void * @var{k}, size_t @var{ksize}, const void * @var{v}, size_t @var{vsize})
Insert or replace a key--value pair.  The @cfunc{sp_set} function is
used to insert new key, or to replace an existing key, in a database.

Example:

@example
int key = 1234;
char value[128];
rc = sp_set(db, &key, sizeof(key), value, sizeof(value));
if (rc == -1) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_set} returns @code{0}; on error, it returns
@code{-1}.  See @cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_get (void * @var{db}, const void * @var{k}, size_t @var{ksize}, void ** @var{v}, size_t * @var{vsize})
Find a key in a database.  The @cfunc{sp_get} function is used to find
keys in a database.  If a key is found, it's value and value size is
copied and returned through the supplied arguments.  Value must be
@cfunc{free}'d after a use.

If no value pointer is supplied, @cfunc{sp_get} is used only to check
for the existence of a key.

Example:

@example
int key = 1234;
void *value;
size_t vsize;
int rc;
rc = sp_get(db, &key, sizeof(key), &value, &vsize);
switch (rc) @{
       case -1:
            printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
            break;
       case  0:
             printf("no key found\n");
             break;
       case  1:
             printf("key found\n");
             /* ... */
             /* free value */
             break;
@}
@end example

On a key found, @cfunc{sp_get} returns @code{1}; on a key not found,
@cfunc{sp_get} returns @code{0}; on error, it returns @code{-1}.  See
@cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_delete (void * @var{db}, const void * @var{k}, size_t @var{ksize})
Delete key from a database.  The @cfunc{sp_delete} function is used to
delete key in a database.

Example:

@example
int key = 1234;
rc = sp_delete(db, &key, sizeof(key));
if (rc == -1) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_delete} returns @code{0}; on error, it returns
@code{-1}.  See @cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun {void *} sp_cursor (void * @var{db}, sporder @var{o}, const void * @var{k}, size_t @var{ksize})
Create a database cursor.  The @cfunc{sp_cursor} function is used to
create cursor handle for further key iteration in a specific order.  If
a key is supplied, the cursor is positioned to the key position, if any.

Possible order are:

@table @code
@item SPGT
Increasing order (skipping the key, if it is equal).

@item SPGTE
Increasing order (with key).

@item SPLT
Decreasing order (skippng the key, if is is equal).

@item SPLTE
Decreasing order.
@end table

After a use, cursor handle should be freed by @cfunc{sp_destroy}
function.

To iterate a cursor, see @cfunc{sp_fetch} function.

Example:

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
/* do cursor iteration */
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_cursor} the allocated cursor object; on error, it
returns @cnull{}.  See @cfunc{sp_error} for details.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun int sp_fetch (void * @var{cursor})
Iterate a cursor.  The @cfunc{sp_fetch} function is used to make a
single step of cursor iteration in a specific order.  Current key and
it's value can be accessed through @cfunc{sp_key}, @cfunc{sp_keysize},
@cfunc{sp_value}, @cfunc{sp_valuesize}) functions accordingly.

Example:

@example
/* iterate all database keys in increasing order and
   count keys. */
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
int count = 0;
while (sp_fetch(c))
   count++;
printf("count: %d\n", count);
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On more keys, @cfunc{sp_fetch} returns @code{1}; on last key,
@cfunc{sp_fetch} returns @code{0}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun {const char *} sp_key (void * @var{cursor})
Get current key.  The @cfunc{sp_key} function is used to access the key
from where a cursor is currently positioned.

Example:

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
   printf("key: %s\n", sp_key(c));
   printf("key size: %zu\n", sp_keysize(c));
@}
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_key} returns a key pointer; on error,
@cfunc{sp_key} returns @cnull{}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun size_t sp_keysize (void * @var{cursor})
Get current key size.  The @cfunc{sp_keysize} function is used to access
currently positioned key size by a cursor.

Example:

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
   printf("key: %s\n", sp_key(c));
   printf("key size: %d\n", sp_keysize(c));
@}
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_keysize} returns the key size; on error,
@cfunc{sp_keysize} function returns @code{0}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun {const char *} sp_value (void * @var{cursor})
Get current cursor value.  The @cfunc{sp_value} function is used to
access the key from where the cursor is currently positioned.

Example:

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
   printf("value: %s\n", sp_value(c));
   printf("value size: %d\n", sp_valuesize(c));
@}
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_value} returns a value pointer; on error, it
returns @cnull{}.
@end deftypefun


@deftypefun size_t sp_valuesize (void * @var{cursor})
Get current cursor value size.  The @cfunc{sp_valuesize} function is
used to retrieve the size of the key from where the cursor is currently
positioned.

Example:

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGT, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
   printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(db));
@}
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
   printf("value: %s\n", sp_value(c));
   printf("value size: %d\n", sp_valuesize(c));
@}
sp_destroy(c);
@end example

On success, @cfunc{sp_valuesize} returns the value size; on error, it
returns @code{0}.
@end deftypefun

